cff-version: 1.2.0
title: NEMSweep
message: >-
  If you use NEMSweep in work you publish, please cite the run rather than the
  tool: "Modelled with NEMSweep <version> at commit <commit>, scenario <scenario
  config>, run <runId>." A result is reproducible only against a specific
  version, commit and scenario. `nemsweep --version` reports the version and the
  result carries `provenance.gitCommitSha` and `runId`, but no dispatch result
  records which scenario config produced it, so name that file yourself.
  Citation is how a method gets scrutinised rather than just used.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Hasintha
    family-names: Attanayake
repository-code: 'https://github.com/HasinthaAttanayake/NEMSweep'
url: 'https://www.nemsweep.com/'
abstract: >-
  An hourly grid dispatch, storage sizing, cost and emissions model for
  electricity systems. Region identifiers are free-form, and the published
  example is Australia's National Electricity Market. Regions are described with
  their demand, generation assets and storage, then dispatched hour by hour
  across a modelled year; where a declared reliability standard is not met,
  storage is grown until it is met or the search reports what stopped it. The
  model holds no cost, demand, weather or carbon figures of its own: every one
  of them is supplied by whoever runs it. Deterministic: the same inputs at the
  same commit reproduce every modelled value, and every result records the
  SHA-256 of the input bytes it was built from.
keywords:
  - electricity
  - National Electricity Market
  - dispatch model
  - energy systems
  - renewable energy
  - storage sizing
  - emissions
  - Australia
license: BSD-3-Clause
# There is no DOI, and that is a decision rather than an omission. Minting one
# means archiving a snapshot of this repository permanently and publicly, and the
# snapshot would include the demand, generation and weather artifacts published
# with the results site. Those derive from AEMO and EnergyPlus Weather sources
# whose redistribution terms are still open; see DATA-LICENSE.md. An
# archive is deliberately hard to withdraw, so it is the wrong place to resolve
# that question after the fact.
